  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Public adjusters and investment planning connect in one simple way: if property is a big part of your net worth, a badly handled insurance claim can wipe out years of equity growth. A licensed public adjuster represents you, the policyholder, on a property claim, which can help make sure a major loss is documented and paid fairly. For real estate investors and homeowners, that belongs in the same conversation as reserves, insurance and long-term planning.</p>

<h2>What a public adjuster is (and is not)</h2>
<p>When you file a property claim, the insurance company assigns its own adjuster, either an employee or an independent adjuster it hires. That adjuster works for the insurer. A public adjuster is different: you hire them, and they work on your side of the claim.</p>
<p>A public adjuster typically:</p>
<ul>
<li>Reviews your policy to identify the coverage that may apply</li>
<li>Inspects and documents the damage with photos, measurements and estimates</li>
<li>Prepares and submits the claim package</li>
<li>Negotiates with the insurance company's adjuster</li>
</ul>
<p>A public adjuster is not an attorney and cannot give legal advice. If a claim turns into a coverage dispute or a lawsuit, you need a lawyer. Bankrate has a helpful consumer overview of <a href="https://www.bankrate.com/insurance/homeowners-insurance/hiring-a-public-adjuster/">when hiring a public adjuster makes sense</a>.</p>

<h2>Licensing and fees in Texas</h2>
<p>In Texas, public adjusters must be licensed by the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/">Texas Department of Insurance (TDI)</a>. Before you sign a contract, look up the adjuster's license on the TDI site and confirm it is active.</p>
<p>Public adjusters are usually paid a percentage of the settlement, spelled out in a written contract. Texas law caps that fee and sets rules for the contract, including a short window to cancel after signing. Read the contract carefully and confirm the current limits with TDI rather than relying on what any one firm tells you.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Be wary of anyone who knocks on your door right after a storm offering to "handle everything." Ask for their TDI license number, check it yourself, and never sign a contract on the spot.</p></div>

<h2>Why this matters for investment planning</h2>
<h3>Real estate is often your largest asset</h3>
<p>For many families, a home and maybe a rental or two make up most of their net worth. If a hailstorm, fire or burst pipe causes major damage and the claim is underpaid, the shortfall comes straight out of your equity or your savings.</p>
<h3>Rental income stops when units are unlivable</h3>
<p>For investors, a damaged rental can mean lost rent on top of repair costs. Some policies include loss-of-rents or fair rental value coverage, but only if the claim includes it and it is documented properly. Missing that can turn a manageable event into a cash flow problem.</p>
<h3>Settlements fund rebuilding decisions</h3>
<p>The settlement amount determines whether you rebuild, upgrade, sell or hold. A thorough claim gives you more options. If you are weighing a rebuild, our post on <a href="/rebuilding-apartment-units-appraisal-for-total-loss-claims/">appraisal for total loss claims</a> explains how disputes over the amount of loss are resolved.</p>

<h2>Building claims readiness into your plan</h2>
<p>The best time to prepare for a claim is long before you need one. A few habits pay off:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Review your policies every year.</strong> Know your deductibles (wind and hail deductibles in Texas are often a percentage of dwelling coverage), coverage limits and whether you have replacement cost or actual cash value.</li>
<li><strong>Document condition now.</strong> Take dated photos and video of every room, roof and system. Update after renovations.</li>
<li><strong>Keep records together.</strong> Policies, receipts, inspection reports and leases should be easy to find. A [[amz:document_organizer|document organizer]] and a [[amz:fireproof_safe|fireproof safe]] help keep originals safe.</li>
<li><strong>Carry reserves.</strong> Even a well-paid claim takes time. Cash reserves cover the deductible and the gap before money arrives.</li>
<li><strong>Know who you would call.</strong> Identify a contractor, an attorney and a licensed public adjuster before disaster strikes.</li>
</ol>
<!--products:fireproof_safe,document_organizer,water_leak|Simple ways to be claim-ready-->
<p>Detecting problems early also limits damage. Inexpensive [[amz:water_leak|water leak sensors]] near water heaters and under sinks can catch a slow leak before it becomes a full claim.</p>

<h2>Questions to ask before hiring one</h2>
<p>If you decide outside help makes sense, interview more than one firm. Useful questions include:</p>
<ul>
<li>What is your TDI license number, and who will actually handle my claim?</li>
<li>What is your fee, and is it calculated on the full settlement or only on amounts above the insurer's first offer?</li>
<li>How many claims like mine, by property type and cause of loss, have you handled?</li>
<li>How will you keep me updated, and how long do claims like this usually take?</li>
<li>Do you have any relationship with contractors you may recommend?</li>
</ul>
<p>Get the answers in writing, and make sure the contract matches what you were told.</p>

<h2>When to consider a public adjuster</h2>
<p>A public adjuster tends to add the most value when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The loss is large, such as major storm, fire or flood-related water damage</li>
<li>The damage is complex, involving structure, contents and loss of use or rents</li>
<li>You lack time or experience to document everything yourself</li>
<li>The insurer's initial estimate looks far below what contractors are quoting</li>
</ul>
<p>For a small claim close to your deductible, the fee may not be worth it. Texas firms such as <a href="https://txpublicadjusting.com/">TX Public Adjusting</a> can review a claim and tell you whether outside help is likely to make a difference. Weigh the fee against the realistic improvement in your settlement.</p>
<p>This article is general information, not legal, insurance or financial advice. Every policy and claim is different, so read your own policy and consult licensed professionals.</p>

